Vitamin D Deficiency
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Diffuse bone pain, Proximal muscle weakness, Bone tenderness on palpation, Low serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-049
Reason for Visit: Severe exacerbation of deep lower back and pelvic pain, severe muscle spasms, and profound generalized weakness limiting mobility.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe deterioration in mobility caused by unmanageable, diffuse bone pain and agonizing muscle cramps. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude acute pathological fracture, provide potent analgesia, and determine if the severe cramping represented critical symptomatic hypocalcaemia before standard clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back and hips are aching so deeply I can't stand straight, my legs are cramping violently, and I have absolutely no strength."
History of Present Illness: Over the past several months, the patient describes an insidious onset of generalized muscle weakness and fatigue. Recently, they developed diffuse, poorly localized bone pain, which is worst in the lower back, pelvis, and lower extremities. Today, the pain escalated severely, rendering the patient unable to rise from a chair without extreme difficulty and upper body assistance. The patient reports severe proximal muscle myopathy resulting in a waddling gait when they attempt to walk. Over the last 12 hours, they developed severe muscle spasms and cramps in the calves and hands. They report a profoundly depressed mood and impaired wound healing of minor cuts.
Functional Status: Chair-bound tonight due to severe pelvic pain and proximal weakness; highly distressed by the muscle cramping.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk due to myopathy and unstable gait. History of a restricted indoor lifestyle due to depression.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 85 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99 percent on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Fatigued, guarding the pelvis, and visibly wincing when attempting to adjust posture.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe bone tenderness upon firm palpation of the sternum and bilateral anterior tibiae. Neurological exam confirms symmetrical, profound proximal muscle weakness in the lower limbs (power 3/5). Mild hyperreflexia is noted in the distal extremities, and a mildly positive Chvostek's sign (facial twitching) is elicited, suggesting secondary hypocalcaemia driving the acute spasms. Range of motion in the hips is globally painful but without shortening or external rotation to suggest an acute displaced fracture. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Diffuse bone pain, Proximal muscle weakness, Bone tenderness on palpation, and anticipated severely Low serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D pending laboratory confirmation.
Environmental Observations: House is dark with all blinds drawn; patient admits to rarely leaving the house over the past year.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular prescribed medications. Over-the-counter paracetamol provides minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Vitamin D Deficiency presenting with osteomalacic bone pain and symptomatic hypocalcaemic spasms - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Lumbar Disc Herniation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is diffuse across the pelvis and sternum rather than radiating in a sharp, dermatomal (sciatic) distribution, and the weakness is symmetrical and proximal rather than focal.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exquisite bony tenderness directly on the sternum and tibia, combined with the objective findings of proximal myopathy and latent tetany (Chvostek's sign), which point to a definitive organic metabolic failure.

Multiple Myeloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound history of sun deprivation and the specific presence of hypocalcaemic spasms strongly favor severe nutritional/environmental deficiency, though serum protein electrophoresis may be checked routinely.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute functional decline driven by severe osteomalacic bone pain and neuromuscular irritability (spasms) secondary to suspected profound vitamin D deficiency and subsequent hypocalcaemia. Triage accurately flagged the inability to mobilize and severe cramping. Objective assessment confirmed the proximal weakness, bone tenderness, and latent tetany. Delaying assessment forced the patient to remain in severe pain and risked a traumatic fall. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to provide bridging analgesia, assess for impending severe tetany, and coordinate an urgent diagnostic and replacement strategy, directly fulfilling urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral ibuprofen 400mg and paracetamol 1g for the acute bone pain. Provided an oral calcium supplement (1g) from the emergency bag to acutely address the latent tetany and muscle cramping. Arranged an urgent comprehensive fasting blood panel for tomorrow morning, including serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D, ionized calcium, phosphate, PTH, and ALP.
Positive Behavioral Change: By deliberately consuming a high-calcium snack (like yogurt or cheese) at the same time you take your future vitamin D supplements (action), you ensure the active vitamin D has an immediate supply of calcium in the gut to absorb (cause), directly speeding up the repair of your softened bones (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Walking stick provided for immediate stability during indoor transfers.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 72 hours to interpret the metabolic panel and initiate high-dose cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) replacement therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops severe, painful locking of the hands or feet, profound numbness around the mouth, or a sudden, severe, localized pain in the hip or groin, present to the emergency department immediately or call 000.

